CrashLoopBackoff -  (When repeated restarts happen with increasing delays, we see CrashLoopBackOff)

BackOff?
Suppose your application container starts but crashes immediately. Without a backoff mechanism, Kubernetes would continuously restart it:
Start → Crash → Restart → Crash → Restart → Crash

This can consume CPU, generate excessive logs and put unnecessary pressure on the worker node.

So K8S Increases the waiting time between restart attempts.

How does it work?
CrashLoopBackOff timeline
1. First attempt
   
   Container starts → Application crashes.
2. Second attempt
   
   Kubernetes waits approximately 10 seconds before restarting.
3. Third attempt
   
   Container crashes again → waits approximately 20 seconds.
4. Fourth attempt
   
   Crashes again → waits approximately 40 seconds.
5. Subsequent attempts
   
   Waiting time continues increasing, up to approximately 5 minutes by default.



What happens internally?

Flow

Kubelet detects container stopped
              |
              v
Kubelet checks restart policy
              |
              v
Restart container
              |
              v
Application crashes again
              |
              v
Increase backoff delay
              |
              v
Wait before next restart
              |
              v
CrashLoopBackOff


Troubleshoot Commands

#	Command	What we investigate
1	kubectl get pods -n dev	Pod status and restart count
2	kubectl describe pod POD -n dev	Events, probes, exit code, container state
3	kubectl logs POD -n dev	Current application logs
4	kubectl logs POD -n dev --previous	Logs from the crashed container
5	kubectl get events -n dev --sort-by=.lastTimestamp	Cluster events and failures
6	kubectl get pod POD -n dev -o yaml	Full pod configuration
7	kubectl top pod POD -n dev	CPU and memory consumption
8	kubectl describe deployment APP -n dev	Deployment configuration and rollout
9	kubectl get configmap -n dev	Available ConfigMaps
10	kubectl get secrets -n dev	Available Secrets
